>
>I know it's not fun to have your basic assumptions challenged.

1) I enjoy having my basic assumptions challenged.  2) This isn't the place
for it.  3) What makes you think your post was challenging?

>What's this reading list you all reference?  Is the 
>Dyson/Hayek/Friedman/Nozick crowd?  I've read and enjoyed them all.

If you've actually read David Friedman's work, then you don't need to ask
about how we propose to organize spaceflight efforts, legal systems, etc.

>...To 
>point is, if you're so keen on their ideas, I want to hear your new 
>thinking.  Why not simply present your objections in rational language, 
>rather than point to some mysterious oracles who have all the answers?

You didn't ask nicely.

>Government agencies can be a great instrument for achieving leverage. 

True.  But power still corrupts.

> They're no more inherently sinister and corrupt than private 
>corporations--probably less so, since most of what they do is visible to 
>most of the populace.

What makes you think that most of what the State does is in fact overt?
How do you know that most of what it does isn't covert?

Aside from that, the difference is that if a private firm runs at a loss,
it eventually goes out of business.  If the State runs at a loss, then it
just raises taxes, issues more bonds, or prints more currency.

>...The "free market" is at least as coercive--setting 
>standards, constraining our choices, cutting off support for outdated 
>products, tightening the intellectual property screws, polluting and gouging 
>the environment--as any government.

You evidently haven't grasped the meaning of coercion around here, which is
used to refer exclusively to the initiation of force/fraud against another.
None of the voluntary things you've mentioned above involve that, and all of
the involuntary things are opposed by most of us because they are coercive.
But, to the extent that firms engage in coercion, the market isn't free,
so the exception just goes to show that the rule doesn't apply.

