From: dasher@netcom.com (Anton Sherwood)
Date: Sat, 29 Oct 1994 09:03:48 -0700
Subject: [#94-10-598] fwd: ethnic purity for trees

from The Economist, October 22, page 101

MORALLY PINELESS

Scotland is an enlightened country; but there is a nasty streak of
chauvinism in its conservationists.  Their worry is centre on the
Scots pine, a stately conifer.  It is not endangered - it is a
species that flourishes all over Europe.  But some conservationists
are worried about the "Scottishness" of some pines.  The result of
their worry looks likely to be money wasted and, in the end, fewer
pines of all sorts.

Once Scotland was largely covered by forests through which the
capercaillie, the pine marten and other fine beasts roamed free.
But thanks to the intervention of man and the grazing of his deer
and sheep just 12,000 hectares (46 square miles) of this original
forest remain - scattered in 80 separate fragments.

They are pretty places.  Preserving and protecting them may add to
the sum of human happiness.  But it is not only the places that are
being preserved; it is also their genetic identity.  Analysis of the
terpenes - organic chemicals in the sap - in Scots pines produces a
different pattern in trees native to Scotland from those overseas.
On the basis of this difference, the trees are now called _Pinus
sylvestris_, variety _scotica_.  And having identified a particular
sub-species, some want to put effort into preserving it.

A harder look at the terpenes reveals regional differences even
within this sub-species.  And it is on the basis of this
micro-discrimination that policies are being put in place to
preserve racial purity among the trees.  Anyone wanting a grant to
plant Scots pines has to use seed collected exclusively from cones
within the zone in which they will be planted.

This ensures that no passing biochemist will be shocked by
mismatched terpenes.  To everyone else it will be completely
invisible.  A Scots pine in Argyll looks no different from one in
Sutherland - or in Lapland.  The pines are found from Spain to the
Caucasus, from Scotland to Siberia, and they all look the same.  Yet
German and Scandinavian Scots pines that have misguidedly ventured
into Scotland are now being felled lest they pollute the local gene
pool.

This obsession with genetic purity may be funnier when applied to
pines than when applied to people; but it is no better justified.
Using strains from elsewhere is often a useful part of a
conservation programme.  Since 1989, Spanish red kites have been
introduced to England, and Swedish kites to Scotland - both
countries kiteless for almost a century.  Meanwhile, the Welsh red
kite population has been brought back from the brink of oblivion.
If all goes according to plan, these three populations will soon be
interbreeding, and the heady mix of Spanish, Swedish and Welsh genes
will probably do some good.  Neither the Government's Joint Nature
Conservation Committee nor the influential Royal Society for the
Preservation of Birds has any doubts that this will be to the birds'
advantage - and will be undetectable to everyone else, since the
sub-species are identical in appearance.  It seems an eminently
sensible attitude: pine heads, please copy.

